{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He served as regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem on multiple occasions due to the young age or incapacity of various kings. His life was marked by complex political alliances and constant military threats from Muslim forces led by Saladin. Raymond belonged to a powerful family that held significant influence over the Crusader states in the Levant region.\n\n*Early Life and Family Background*\nRaymond was born around the year 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the sister of King Fulk of Jerusalem which gave him royal blood ties. He inherited the County of Tripoli at a very young age after his father was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. This tragic event forced him to grow up under the protection of King Baldwin III. Raymond spent his youth learning the arts of war and governance while navigating the treacherous political landscape of the Crusader kingdoms. He later married Eschiva of Bures to secure additional lands and influence within the region.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count assumed the role of regent for Baldwin IV after the king contracted leprosy as a child. This position placed him at the center of power struggles between various factions within the court. He often clashed with Guy of Lusignan who married Sibylla the sister of the king. Raymond aligned himself with the Ibelin family to oppose the aggressive policies of Guy and his supporters. He also sought an alliance with Saladin at times to preserve the fragile peace between the Christian and Muslim realms. His diplomatic maneuvers were often misunderstood as treason by other Crusader leaders who wanted immediate confrontation.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Death*\nRaymond led several military expeditions against Muslim forces throughout his long 